dsh-memory — Debian/Ubuntu package notes
========================================

What this package installs
--------------------------
  /usr/lib/dsh/plugins/memory/   the plugin package root (lib/)
  /usr/share/doc/dsh-memory/     this note, copyright, changelog,
                                 BUILD-INFO.json, and docs/

The package root contains package.json, cordis.patch.yml, and lib/ (the
compiled Host entry, its declarations, and the browser bundle), plus locale/,
icon.svg, README/LICENSE, and docs/. The docs are also copied under
/usr/share/doc/dsh-memory/docs/. That directory is the unit DeepSeek Harness
installs: the Web Plugins page ("Add plugin") and `dsh plugin add <path>` both
read it. The plugin has no runtime npm dependencies; every DeepSeek Harness
package is type-only at build time and provided by the Host.

The package does not modify user profiles and does not start a service. It
also does not install Node.js or DeepSeek Harness itself; it Depends on
deepseek-harness (>= @DSH_MIN_VERSION@).

Enabling the plugin (per user, not with sudo)
---------------------------------------------
  dsh plugin --profile web add /usr/lib/dsh/plugins/memory

Or, in the Web UI: Plugins -> Add plugin -> enter
/usr/lib/dsh/plugins/memory.

Installation enables the bundle in the profile. A running Host picks it up
through HMR when the profile uses live reload; otherwise restart the Web UI:

  systemctl --user restart dsh-web

Do not run these commands with sudo: the profile belongs to the user, and
root-owned files under ~/.dsh break the user service. A Host plugin runs
in-process with that user's privileges and can read its credentials, so
install only packages you trust.

Usage
-----
After enabling and restarting, the agent gets eight memory tools and every
session receives the project memory index. With the Web client, each injection
renders as its own Chat row, and `/memory` opens the management panel with a
Project / Global switch.

  memory_write, memory_read, memory_search, memory_delete,
  memory_audit, memory_sweep, memory_restore, memory_dream

Project notes live in $DSH_HOME/memory/<project>-<hash>/; user-global notes
live in $DSH_HOME/memory/global/. Archived notes are kept for 30 days by
default (`archiveMaxAgeDays`); `memory_sweep` reports them in a dry run and
removes them only when applied.

The row configuration in the profile's cordis.patch.yml accepts the index
budgets, search limits, audit thresholds, archive retention, and Auto Dream
settings. See /usr/share/doc/dsh-memory/docs/DETAIL.md.

Upgrading
---------
`apt install` replaces the directory in place. The profile links to the
directory, not to a version, so no reinstall is needed; restart the Web UI to
load the new code (or let HMR reload it).

Removing
--------
Remove the bundle from every profile that enabled it first:

  dsh plugin --profile web remove @PLUGIN_NAME@

Then remove the package:

  sudo apt remove dsh-memory

Removing the package first leaves a dangling link in the profile. DeepSeek
Harness then reports the bundle as skipped, and the `dsh plugin remove`
command above still clears it. `apt purge` removes only the files listed in
the package; memory notes under ~/.dsh/memory/ are user data and are not
touched.

